uch money coming  from the federal government  and from large investors into  the resilience economy into the disaster  rebuilding industry but at the bottom  there are very few protections for these  workers so they suffer wage theft  often when they ask for bathroom breaks  or the right to  safety equipment  they're let go  if they protest those abuses  immigration is often called on them um  you know this is not a work environment  in which these workers are thriving even  though the work is as necessary and  essential as it is right and you filed  class actual lawsuits on behalf of these  workers and actually won them back pay  in the process but doing so really pits  the workers against the companies that  hire them or in this case the  subcontractors and it doesn't really get  to the heart of the problem which is  building accountability from uh into the  industry from the top all the way down  to the smallest subcontractor how do you  go about doing that  you're absolutely right and that's why  we're calling on the investors  of these companies the people who invest  in them and profit from  um the viability of these companies to  push these companies  to sign on to our standards  the  companies like servpro  who who have been repeat offenders  servpro is a company we literally get  litigated against numerous times because  of the abuses of its franchisees and its  subcontractors  rather than spending that time  uh  in court rather than eating up a judge's  time  um we'd much rather invite these  companies to embrace a set of standards  um and and um you know comply with those  standards so that homes cities  schools buildings can get rebuilt faster  and more reliably after disasters as  you're talking about this i'm thinking  about how much more complicated the  resilience workers work is during covid  and especially something like omicron  when the virus is so contagious uh  they're all essential workers they're  all front line workers so it's not as if  they can quarantine uh for ten days and  and the work can wait um what kind of  existing laws are there to protect these  workers even though many of them are  undocumented they're migrant workers and  um they're not neces the subcontractors  they work for aren't necessarily  held to the same standards as as larger  companies  the laws are very weak and  you know what laws exist aren't worth  the paper they exist on because there is  very little enforcement i remember in um  the incredibly severe hurricane season  we had last year i was with workers in  louisiana  working at the bottom end of  big contracts repairing hundreds and  hundreds of  homes roofs entire school districts and  these workers were telling us that when  they contracted covid and told their  supervisors they needed day off or they  would infect their colleagues their  co-workers um  the  the subcontractors wouldn't allow it you  know the supervisors of these work sites  um we've found very frequently forced  people to work despite the fact that  they have coveted  very often refuse  requests for ppe  and safety equipment uh when workers are  symptomatic they're refused the  opportunity to get tested again and  again we've seen um  and this was in fact the subject of um  our litigation  against servpro where workers at the  bottom of that contracting chain found  themselves with kovid while building  michigan um  hospitals after uh two big dam breaks  last year um and you know they weren't  allowed to quarantine they weren't  allowed to  um to take time off from work and  eventually they were let go rather than  given the chance to heal and go back to  work and finish what they came for i see  well when we talk about solutions beyond  litigation which as you said takes up a  lot of time and doesn't necessarily  solve the problem you have launched a  pilot program in new orleans which of  course is home to far too many storms  and disaster what does that program do  and how can it be a blueprint for  something  that we can see in other cities  well in new orleans in partnership with  the city  and other stakeholders  and really because of the leadership of  the mayor we've partnered with the city  and  taken  low wage minimum wage service industry  workers who were laid off  because of covid people who lost their  jobs  in the  unemployment crisis that came after the  pandemic  and we retrained them and we turned them  into  resilience workers and we built a  resilience core  that faced into the covid disaster and  the climate disaster  we think that core should spread it  should spread to  places like california where the  wildfires require it  places like kentucky and colorado where  flooding tornadoes and um  uh  you know and and and other severe  weather events um  create a need for workers to meet  construction needs and human needs  and you know as the united states looks  at how to build a civilian conservation  corps the resilience corps in new  orleans is a great model you take people  who are out of work you retrain them you  turn them into people uh who will work  year round making buildings and  communities safe  before the next disaster and repair  buildings and communities  after disasters hit yeah you can solve  more than one problem at a time that way  socket really appreciate your taking the  time to explain everything you do socket  sony is founder and director of  resilience force
 

